WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.979
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:08.630
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:16.157
Unit twenty-seven: Logic and Paradox. Today you will concede a point using however + adjective clauses.

4
00:00:16.457 --> 00:00:19.040
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.047
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.883 --> 00:00:24.803
Logic.

7
00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:28.176
Logic means the study of valid reasoning.

8
00:00:28.783 --> 00:00:32.223
Logic distinguishes a valid argument from a persuasive one.

9
00:00:32.817 --> 00:00:35.724
Your turn. Say: Logic.

10
00:00:38.567 --> 00:00:39.486
Logic.

11
00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:41.298
Paradox.

12
00:00:42.283 --> 00:00:46.837
Paradox means a seemingly sound argument that leads to a contradictory conclusion.

13
00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:51.263
The liar paradox arises from the sentence 'This sentence is false.'

14
00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:54.595
Your turn. Say: Paradox.

15
00:00:57.433 --> 00:00:58.585
Paradox.

16
00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:00.339
Premise.

17
00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:04.464
Premise means a statement from which a conclusion is drawn.

18
00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:07.816
A false premise can undermine a valid argument.

19
00:01:08.417 --> 00:01:10.918
Your turn. Say: Premise.

20
00:01:13.767 --> 00:01:14.710
Premise.

21
00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:16.590
Contradiction.

22
00:01:17.583 --> 00:01:21.231
Contradiction means a statement that cannot be true together with another.

23
00:01:21.817 --> 00:01:25.337
A contradiction signals that an assumption must be rejected.

24
00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:28.591
Your turn. Say: Contradiction.

25
00:01:31.433 --> 00:01:32.597
Contradiction.

26
00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:34.351
Validity.

27
00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:39.997
Validity means the property of an argument whose conclusion follows from its premises.

28
00:01:40.583 --> 00:01:43.001
Validity concerns structure, not truth.

29
00:01:43.617 --> 00:01:46.234
Your turn. Say: Validity.

30
00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:50.275
Validity.

31
00:01:51.017 --> 00:01:52.134
Infinity.

32
00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:56.241
Infinity means a quantity without end or limit.

33
00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.918
Cantor showed that some infinities are larger than others.

34
00:02:00.517 --> 00:02:03.018
Your turn. Say: Infinity.

35
00:02:05.867 --> 00:02:06.879
Infinity.

36
00:02:07.617 --> 00:02:09.094
Self-reference.

37
00:02:10.083 --> 00:02:13.163
Self-reference means a statement that refers to itself.

38
00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:17.120
Self-reference lies behind many famous paradoxes.

39
00:02:17.717 --> 00:02:20.880
Your turn. Say: Self-reference.

40
00:02:23.733 --> 00:02:25.245
Self-reference.

41
00:02:25.983 --> 00:02:26.903
Reasoning.

42
00:02:27.917 --> 00:02:31.658
Reasoning means the process of thinking logically to reach a conclusion.

43
00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:34.551
Careful reasoning examines each step.

44
00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:37.791
Your turn. Say: Reasoning.

45
00:02:40.633 --> 00:02:41.530
Reasoning.

46
00:02:42.333 --> 00:02:45.845
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:46.683 --> 00:02:52.668
Visal, every step of this paradox looks correct, but the conclusion is absurd. Is logic broken?

48
00:02:53.783 --> 00:03:00.929
Not broken. However convincing the premise, the conclusion is absurd, so at least one premise must be wrong.

49
00:03:02.017 --> 00:03:03.076
Which one?

50
00:03:04.183 --> 00:03:11.967
That is the work. However careful the reasoning appears, hidden assumptions can lurk, particularly in self-reference.

51
00:03:13.083 --> 00:03:15.164
So paradoxes are failures?

52
00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:24.696
Quite the opposite. However unsettling they are, they expose the limits of our concepts, and that is how logic progresses.

53
00:03:25.833 --> 00:03:27.682
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:28.517 --> 00:03:32.502
Grammar focus: Concessive clauses (However plus adjective).

55
00:03:32.802 --> 00:03:33.988
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:34.683 --> 00:03:37.797
However convincing the premise, the conclusion is absurd.

57
00:03:38.683 --> 00:03:42.506
However careful the reasoning appears, hidden assumptions can lurk.

58
00:03:43.417 --> 00:03:47.622
However unsettling paradoxes are, they expose the limits of concepts.

59
00:03:48.567 --> 00:03:58.475
However + adjective/adverb + subject + verb = no matter how. Keep the adjective right after however: However convincing it is (not 'However it is convincing').

60
00:03:59.217 --> 00:04:04.017
Be careful! Don't say: However the premise is convincing, it is false.

61
00:04:05.967 --> 00:04:09.150
Say: However convincing the premise is, it is false.

62
00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:12.239
However + adjective + subject + verb.

63
00:04:13.033 --> 00:04:15.953
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:16.767 --> 00:04:18.499
When Logic Surprises Us.

65
00:04:18.799 --> 00:04:25.164
Logic is the study of valid reasoning, yet some of its most instructive moments arise when reasoning appears to fail.

66
00:04:26.167 --> 00:04:34.250
A paradox is an argument that proceeds from apparently acceptable premises, by apparently valid steps, to an unacceptable conclusion.

67
00:04:35.233 --> 00:04:43.456
The liar paradox, for example, arises from self-reference: if the sentence 'This sentence is false' is true, then it is false, and vice versa.

68
00:04:44.467 --> 00:04:51.598
However persuasive the individual steps appear, a contradiction shows that some premise or rule of inference must be revised.

69
00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:58.640
Paradoxes have thus driven major advances, from Cantor's theory of infinity to modern computer science.

70
00:04:59.733 --> 00:05:03.874
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:04.717 --> 00:05:07.691
blank convincing the premise, the conclusion is absurd.

72
00:05:07.991 --> 00:05:11.540
A, However. B, Although. C, Despite.

73
00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:16.786
The answer is A: However.

74
00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:21.888
A seemingly sound argument with a contradictory conclusion is a blank.

75
00:05:22.188 --> 00:05:26.295
A, paradox. B, premise. C, validity.

76
00:05:29.833 --> 00:05:31.856
The answer is A: paradox.

77
00:05:33.017 --> 00:05:35.748
A statement that refers to itself shows blank.

78
00:05:36.048 --> 00:05:39.898
A, infinity. B, self-reference. C, reasoning.

79
00:05:43.433 --> 00:05:45.514
The answer is B: self-reference.

80
00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:48.278
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:48.578 --> 00:05:55.645
A, However it is clever, it is wrong.. B, However clever it is, it is wrong.. C, However clever is it, it is wrong..

82
00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:02.361
The answer is B: However clever it is, it is wrong..

83
00:06:03.517 --> 00:06:05.899
According to the reading, paradoxes have…

84
00:06:06.199 --> 00:06:11.095
A, driven major advances. B, ended logic. C, been ignored.

85
00:06:14.633 --> 00:06:17.678
The answer is A: driven major advances.

86
00:06:18.867 --> 00:06:21.995
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:25.279
Why are paradoxes valuable for thinking?

88
00:06:42.083 --> 00:06:43.502
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:43.802 --> 00:07:13.430
Paradoxes are valuable because they reveal hidden problems in assumptions that seem obvious. However convincing each step appears, the absurd conclusion tells us that something must change: a premise, a definition or a rule. Resolving a paradox often forces us to sharpen concepts, as happened with infinity and self-reference. They are warnings that intuition is not infallible.

90
00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:15.381
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:16.283 --> 00:07:21.583
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and concessive clauses (however plus adjective).

92
00:07:21.883 --> 00:07:27.124
Today's value is logical rigor. Follow your reasoning wherever it honestly leads.

93
00:07:27.424 --> 00:07:30.111
See you in Unit twenty-eight. Goodbye!

94
00:07:30.411 --> 00:07:32.959
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

